== helios edge origin-pull mint (hidden false)
Make the certificate Cloudflare presents for a host, trust its CA, and write the certificate and its key to a new file.
Make the certificate Cloudflare presents for a host, trust its CA, and
write the certificate and its key to a new file.

The CA and the certificate it signs last a year. The edge trusts the CA,
the file gets the certificate and then its key, readable by you alone, and
the CA's key is thrown away: nothing can sign another certificate the edge
takes, and the file holds the only copy of the key. Upload it to
Cloudflare, then delete it. With --target, the certificate is made here and
only the CA goes to the target.

Mint again to replace a certificate. The old CA stays trusted beside the
new one, so the host keeps working while Cloudflare switches. Once
Cloudflare presents the new certificate, run helios edge origin-pull forget
<host> <old fingerprint>: helios can't see which certificate Cloudflare
presents, so an old CA left to run out raises a critical alert.
Usage:
  helios edge origin-pull mint <host> [flags]

Flags:
      --out string   the new file for the certificate and its key

Global Flags:
      --json                  print JSON, one object per line
      --socket string         the daemon's socket; the local kernel is used when nothing answers (default "/run/bedrock/bedrock.sock")
      --state-dir string      where helios keeps its state (default "~/.bedrock")
      --target string         operate on this explicitly named SSH target
      --targets-file string   the local named-target configuration (default "~/.bedrock/targets.json")
== helios edge origin-pull trust (hidden false)
Trust a CA's certificate for a host, from a file or stdin, as on a machine taking a host over.
Trust a CA's certificate for a host, from a file or stdin, as on a machine
taking a host over. The certificate is the CA's that helios edge origin-pull
mint made, as helios edge origin-pull list --json shows it, never the one
uploaded to Cloudflare.

  helios edge origin-pull trust shop.example.com ca.pem
Usage:
  helios edge origin-pull trust <host> [file] [flags]

Global Flags:
      --json                  print JSON, one object per line
      --socket string         the daemon's socket; the local kernel is used when nothing answers (default "/run/bedrock/bedrock.sock")
      --state-dir string      where helios keeps its state (default "~/.bedrock")
      --target string         operate on this explicitly named SSH target
      --targets-file string   the local named-target configuration (default "~/.bedrock/targets.json")
== helios exec (hidden false)
Run a command inside a workload's container.
Run a command inside a workload's container.

At a terminal, the command reads what is typed there. Anywhere else, as in
a script piped to bash -s, the command's standard input is empty, so the
script keeps its next lines. -i hands the command this standard input.

  helios exec site -- sh
  helios exec -i site -- sh -c 'cat > /data/import.csv' < import.csv
Usage:
  helios exec <app> [workload] -- <command...> [flags]

Flags:
  -i, --interactive   keep standard input open for the command, as docker exec -i does

Global Flags:
      --json                  print JSON, one object per line
      --socket string         the daemon's socket; the local kernel is used when nothing answers (default "/run/bedrock/bedrock.sock")
      --state-dir string      where helios keeps its state (default "~/.bedrock")
      --target string         operate on this explicitly named SSH target
      --targets-file string   the local named-target configuration (default "~/.bedrock/targets.json")
