== helios launch (hidden false)
Take a repository from nothing to running here.
Take a repository from nothing to running here.

Fetches the repository, works out what it is, writes the manifest it is
missing and deploys it. A repository that already has a helios.yaml is not
launched: deploy it instead, because overwriting a manifest somebody wrote
with a guess is worse than refusing.

  helios launch https://github.com/you/site --host site.example.com
  helios launch git@github.com:you/api.git --host api.example.com --postgres

What it can work out on its own is deliberately narrow, because a wrong
guess becomes a manifest someone has to debug:

  a Dockerfile            a web service built from it
  public/index.html       a static site from that directory
  index.html at the root  a static site from the root

Anything else is refused with a suggestion, rather than guessed at. The
manifest it writes is an ordinary one: read it, change it, commit it.
Usage:
  helios launch <repository> [flags]

Flags:
      --app string      the app's name (default: the repository's name)
      --branch string   the branch to launch (default "main")
      --digest string   apply only if the plan is still the one with this digest; implies --yes
      --host string     the hostname it answers on
      --keep            leave the fetched tree and the manifest helios wrote behind after a --plan or a failure, to read them
      --plan            show the plan and change nothing
      --port int        the port a web app listens on (default 8000)
      --postgres        give the app a database, and DATABASE_URL to reach it
      --yes             apply without asking

Global Flags:
      --json                  print JSON, one object per line
      --socket string         the daemon's socket; the local kernel is used when nothing answers (default "/run/bedrock/bedrock.sock")
      --state-dir string      where helios keeps its state (default "~/.bedrock")
      --target string         operate on this explicitly named SSH target
      --targets-file string   the local named-target configuration (default "~/.bedrock/targets.json")
