== helios move (hidden false)
Move an app, and its data, to another machine.
Move an app, and its data, to another machine.

The staged workflow first rehearses a capture, then freezes source writers for
an exact final snapshot. The target restores and checks that snapshot before
an explicit write-authority and DNS cutover.

  on the source: helios move rehearse <app> --to target-name > rehearsal.json
  on the target: helios move test <source-dir> --handoff rehearsal.json
  on the source: helios move final <app> --to target-name > final.json
  on the target: helios move stage <source-dir> --handoff final.json
  on the target: helios move cutover <app>

The source stays frozen after final capture, including after a host or daemon
restart. Resume it only deliberately, after stopping destination writers:

  on the source: helios move resume <app> --target-stopped

Destination writes are not included in the source snapshot. Returning those
writes requires a reverse move; an old source is not a lossless rollback.
Import the app's original secrets with move secrets before staging.

The older out/in commands remain for manual copy workflows. Their source
keeps running after capture and can accept writes the copied snapshot lacks.

The backup bucket is the channel between the machines. Both machines must be pointed at the same storage: the
same bucket prefix and the same backup password. A machine reading its own
bucket instead of the source's finds nothing there, which looks like an app
with no data rather than like a failure.

  on the source:  helios move out <app> --yes > handoff.json
  on the target:  helios move in <app> --handoff handoff.json
                  helios deploy <source>
                  helios dns point <host>          # once it answers
  on the source:  helios remove <app>              # after a rollback window

These legacy commands provide a manual copy, without a durable source write
fence or automatic cutover. Quiesce writers before a final manual transfer.
Usage:
  helios move [command]

Available Commands:
  cutover     Recheck the staged target, admit its writers and move production routes and DNS.
  final       Fence all source writers and capture the final handoff; the source stays stopped.
  in          Restore an app's data here from the handoff the source machine wrote.
  out         Back an app up and write the handoff the target machine reads.
  rehearse    Capture a rehearsal snapshot while the source remains available afterwards.
  resume      Deliberately resume a frozen source after stopping the target.
  secrets     Seal an app's secrets for the target machine, and accept them there.
  stage       Restore the final snapshot and validate the target without public routes or DNS.
  test        Prove a rehearsal snapshot on scratch target data without production activation.

Global Flags:
      --json                  print JSON, one object per line
      --socket string         the daemon's socket; the local kernel is used when nothing answers (default "/run/bedrock/bedrock.sock")
      --state-dir string      where helios keeps its state (default "~/.bedrock")
      --target string         operate on this explicitly named SSH target
      --targets-file string   the local named-target configuration (default "~/.bedrock/targets.json")

Use "helios move [command] --help" for more information about a command.
== helios move cutover (hidden false)
Recheck the staged target, admit its writers and move production routes and DNS.
Recheck the staged target, admit its writers and move production routes and DNS.

This is the explicit write-authority boundary. Once destination writers
start, source data may be stale. A failure leaves the source fenced and the
target intact so the operation can be repaired and retried. Helios never
automatically points production back at a stale source.
Usage:
  helios move cutover <app> [flags]

Flags:
      --digest string   apply only if the plan is still the one with this digest; implies --yes
      --plan            show the plan and change nothing
      --yes             apply without asking

