      --targets-file string   the local named-target configuration (default "~/.bedrock/targets.json")
== helios move secrets (hidden false)
Seal an app's secrets for the target machine, and accept them there.
Seal an app's secrets for the target machine, and accept them there.

A moved app has to arrive with the secrets it left with. Its database comes
back from a snapshot still holding the role password the source generated, so
a target that generated its own would be handed a database it cannot open.
helios refuses that restore rather than performing it, which is why this
step exists.

  on the target:  helios secret recipient
  on the source:  helios move secrets <app> --to <age1...> > secrets.age
  on the target:  helios move secrets <app> --accept < secrets.age

Only ciphertext is ever printed, sealed to the target's own key, and it stops
being readable ten minutes after it is made. The values never appear in a
terminal, a log or a shell history on either machine.
Usage:
  helios move secrets <app> [flags]

Flags:
      --accept      read a sealed bundle from standard input and store it here
      --to string   the target machine's recipient, from 'helios secret recipient' there

Global Flags:
      --json                  print JSON, one object per line
      --socket string         the daemon's socket; the local kernel is used when nothing answers (default "/run/bedrock/bedrock.sock")
      --state-dir string      where helios keeps its state (default "~/.bedrock")
      --target string         operate on this explicitly named SSH target
      --targets-file string   the local named-target configuration (default "~/.bedrock/targets.json")
== helios move stage (hidden false)
Restore the final snapshot and validate the target without public routes or DNS.
Restore the final snapshot and validate the target without public routes or DNS.

Import the source secrets first with move secrets. The handoff must come from
move final and name this target machine. Staging starts web and static
workloads for direct checks; worker, cron and release jobs wait. Staged
workloads may write destination data, so resuming the source never recovers
those changes. Use move cutover to admit normal destination writers and DNS.
Usage:
  helios move stage <source-dir> [flags]

Flags:
      --digest string     apply only if the plan is still the one with this digest; implies --yes
      --handoff string    the final source handoff, or - for standard input (default "-")
      --manifest string   the source manifest (default "helios.yaml")
      --plan              show the plan and change nothing
      --yes               apply without asking

Global Flags:
      --json                  print JSON, one object per line
      --socket string         the daemon's socket; the local kernel is used when nothing answers (default "/run/bedrock/bedrock.sock")
      --state-dir string      where helios keeps its state (default "~/.bedrock")
      --target string         operate on this explicitly named SSH target
