== helios recover (hidden false)
Recover an exact machine backup privately, or rehearse it in disposable isolation.
Recover an exact machine backup privately, or rehearse it in disposable isolation.

Configure the target's own identity and storage integration first. The source
recovery identity is read from a protected file or stdin, then sealed with the
target identity for resumable operation. It never appears in operation input.

Recovery restores recorded images, sealed secret versions, pinned app data,
host profile and operational history. It starts workloads privately with no
published ports or live routes. A separate cutover changes live traffic after
readiness. --rehearsal skips target state/profile/integrations and removes its
isolated workloads and volumes after the check.

  helios recover old-host --snapshot <id> --identity-file ./recovery.key --plan
  helios recover old-host --snapshot <id> --identity-file ./recovery.key --yes
  helios recover old-host --snapshot <id> --identity-stdin --rehearsal --yes
  helios recover cutover <recovery-id> --plan

The machine snapshot must contain a compatible recovery catalog and workload
image archive. Data recovery restores the app recovery points embedded inside
that machine snapshot, so app retention cannot remove them.
Usage:
  helios recover <source-machine> [flags]
  helios recover [command]

Available Commands:
  abandon     Stop private recovery containers and release its reservation, preserving restored data and history.
  cutover     Route live traffic to a completed private machine recovery after readiness.
  report      Show the recovery's pinned snapshots, private readiness and cutover status.

Flags:
      --digest string          apply only if the plan is still the one with this digest; implies --yes
      --id string              stable recovery ID; defaults to a hash of source, snapshot and mode
      --identity-file string   protected source recovery identity file; never passed to the operation
      --identity-stdin         read source recovery identity from stdin; requires --yes or --digest
      --plan                   show the plan and change nothing
      --rehearsal              prove recovery in disposable internal networks without changing target state, profile or traffic
      --snapshot string        explicit machine snapshot ID (required; latest is not accepted)
      --yes                    apply without asking

Global Flags:
      --json                  print JSON, one object per line
      --socket string         the daemon's socket; the local kernel is used when nothing answers (default "/run/bedrock/bedrock.sock")
      --state-dir string      where helios keeps its state (default "~/.bedrock")
      --target string         operate on this explicitly named SSH target
      --targets-file string   the local named-target configuration (default "~/.bedrock/targets.json")

Use "helios recover [command] --help" for more information about a command.
